wBD0EPgQgADIEIAA0BDUEOgQwBDEEQAQ1BCAAMgAwADIANAAgADMEPgQ0BDAELAAgADAEIAA\\\\\\\\/wAAAAFcAAAABAYOAAAAQwBhAG0AYgByAGkAYQAFBg4AAABDAGEAbQBiAHIAaQBhAAcGDgAAAEMAYQBtAGIAcgBpAGEABgYOAAAAQwBhAG0AYgByAGkAYQAIBBwAAAAWBBwAAAAImQAAAACUAAAAEgQ8BDUEQQRCBDUEIABBBCAAQgQ1BDwEIAA\\/BEAENQQ\\\\/BEAEPgQxBDsENQQ8BDAEIABABDAENwQ9BD4EOQQgAEgEOARABDgEPQRLBCAAOgQ+BDsENQQ4BCAAKAAxADQAMwA1ACAAPAQ8BCAAMgQgABoEOARCBDAENQQgAD8EQAQ+BEIEOAQyBCAAMQA1ADIAMAAgADwEPAQgAD0EMAQgAD8EPgRBBEIEQQQ+BDIENQRCBEEEOgQ+BDwEIAA\\/BEAEPgRBBEIEQAQwBD0EQQRCBDIENQQpACwAIABHBEIEPgQgAEIEQAQ1BDEEQwQ1BEIEIAA\\/BDUEQAQ1BDMEQARDBDcEPgRHBD0ESwRFBCAAPgQ\\\\\\\\/BEAEPgQ1BDoEQgQwBDwELAAgADoEPgRCBD4EQARLBDUEIABBBDIETwQ2BEMEQgQgACYENQQ9BEIEQAQwBDsETAQ9BEMETgQgABAENwQ4BE4EIABBBCAAFQQyBEAEPgQ\\/BD4EOQQgAEcENQRABDUENwQgAC4ENgQ9BEsEOQQgABoEMAQyBDoEMAQ3BC4AIAAdBDAEIAASBDAESAQgADIENwQzBDsETwQ0BCwAIAA9BDAENwRABDUEOwQwBCAAOwQ4BCAAPQQ1BD4EMQRFBD4ENAQ4BDwEPgRBBEIETAQgADcEMAQ\\\\\\/BD4EQQQ+BDEEPQQ+BCAAPgQ\\/BDgEQAQwBDUEQgRBBE8EIAA9BDAEIAA6BD4EPAQxBDgEPQQwBEYEOAROBCAAPAQ1BEUEMAQ9BDgENwQ8BD4EMgQ6ACAANAQyBEMEQQRCBD4EQAQ+BD0EPQQ4BDUEIAA8BDUENgQ\\\\/AQAAAVwAAAAEBg4AAABDAGEAbQBiAHIAaQBhAAUGDgAAAEMAYQBtAGIAcgBpAGEABwYOAAAAQwBhAG0AYgByAGkAYQAGBg4AAABDAGEAbQBiAHIAaQBhAAgEHAAAABYEHAAAAAgZAQAAABQBAAAwBCAAQgQwBDoENgQ1BCAAPwQ7BD4ESQQwBDQEOgQ4BCAANAQ4BDAEOwQ+BDMEMAQgAEEEIAAyBD0ENQRIBD0EOAQ8BDgEIAA\\/BDAEQARCBD0EUQRABDAEPAQ4BCAAFCAgABUEMgRABD4EPwQ1BDkEQQQ6BDgEPAQgAEEEPgROBDcEPgQ8BCwAIAAiBEMEQARGBDgENQQ5BCwAIAAaBDgEQgQwBDUEPAQuACAALQRCBDgEIAA4BD0EQQRCBEAEQwQ8BDUEPQRCBEsEIAA\\\\/BDUEIAA7BD4EMwQ4BEcEPQQ1BDUEIAA9BDUEIAA\\/BD4ENAQ8BDUEPQRPBEIETAQsACAAMAQgADQEPgRBBEIEQAQwBDgEMgQwBEIETAQgAEEEQwRJBDUEQQRCBDIEQwROBEkEQwROBCAAMARABEUEOARCBDUEOgRCBEMEQARDBCAAFCAgAD0EMAQ\\/BEAEOAQ8BDUEQAQsACAARwQ1BEAENQQ3BCAAQAQ1BDMEQwQ7BE8EQAQ9BEsENQQgADoEPgQ9BEEEQwQ7BEwEQgQwBEYEOAQ4BCAAPQQwBCAAQwRABD4EMgQ9BDUEIAA\\/BEAEPgREBDgEOwRMBD0ESwRFBAVrAgAAAVwAAAAEBg4AAABDAGEAbQBiAHIAaQBhAAUGDgAAAEMAYQBtAGIAcgBpAGEABwYOAAAAQwBhAG0AYgByAGkAYQAGBg4AAABDAGEAbQBiAHIAaQBhAAgEHAAAABYEHAAAAAgFAgAAAAACAAAgADIENQQ0BD4EPARBBEIEMgQgADgEIAA6BD4EPgRABDQEOAQ9BDAERgQ4BE4EIABCBEAEMAQ9BEEEPwQ+BEAEQgQ9BD4ELQBCBDAEQAQ4BEQEPQQ+BDkEIAA\\\\\\\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><b>ÔÇö Uzbekistan and Georgia have brought relations to the level of strategic cooperation. Tell me, what strategic interests does Uzbekistan pursue in the South Caucasus? And what role<\\/b><\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><b><br>&nbsp;<\\/b><\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><b>Can Georgia play in this?<\\/b><\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:34pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">ÔÇö The signing of the Declaration of Strategic Partnership during the July visit cemented that<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"> <\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">which has actually already happened<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">in practice: the South Caucasus has become one of the priority foreign economic directions for Uzbekistan. I would reduce our strategic interests here to three interrelated tasks.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Firstly, this is the diversification of transport routes. Uzbekistan is landlocked, so gaining sustainable access to the Black Sea ports and further to the markets of the European Union<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">and Turkey is of fundamental importance to us. We are talking about reducing dependence on any one transit direction<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">and about integration into new Eurasian logistics chains as their full-fledged participant, and not a peripheral link.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Secondly, it is filling the relationship with economic content. Along with political dialogue, trade, investment, industrial cooperation and transport and logistics are coming to the fore.<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">sky connectedness. It is significant that the parties set the goal of bringing mutual trade turnover to $1 billion, despite the fact that at the end of last year it was about 270 million. This is an ambitious but realistic target that requires systematic work.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Thirdly, this is the expansion of UzbekistanÔÇÖs foreign policy subjectivity outside Central Asia through participation<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">in regional interaction formats.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">The role of Georgia in this coordinate system is quite definite - it is the function of a logistics and transit bridge. Through Georgian territory and the ports of Poti and Batumi, Uzbek exports receive land access to the Black Sea and Europe within the Middle Corridor.<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">It is no coincidence that the Uzbek multifunctional terminal project is already being implemented in the port of Poti - this is the transfer of transit potential from the plane of declarations to the plane of concrete infrastructure.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">In addition, Georgia serves as an additional channel for us to interact with Western partners, including through dialogue<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">with the European Union and the Global Gateway initiatives.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><b>ÔÇö What agreements following the visit do you consider the most significant for the development of Uzbek-Georgian relations?<\\/b><\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">ÔÇö The central result of the visit, of course, was the signing of the Declaration on the Establishment of a Strategic Partnership.<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">This is not a protocol formality, but a qualitative change in the status of relations: for the first time in 23 years, a state visit of this level consolidated the transition from episodic interaction to a systemic partnership with long-term obligations of the parties.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">However, I would caution against assessing the significance of agreements solely by their political ÔÇ£weight.ÔÇØ<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">In my opinion, the most meaningful in the medium term will be those decisions that translate<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">development on a practical level. First of all, this is a transport and logistics block of agreement on the effective use of the potential of the ports of Poti and Batumi and on the development of Middle Corridor routes. They form the economic basis of the partnership.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">No less important is the signed package of industry documents covering the trade and economic sphere, industrial cooperation, pharmaceuticals, energy and digital technologies.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">I would like to separately note the digital direction: in Uzbe<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">The first Georgian digital bank is already operating in Kistan, and direct flights are operating between the countries on the routes Tashkent - Tbilisi - Batumi. This is the infrastructure of everyday interaction, without which any strategic declarations remain on paper.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Others with<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">catches, the greatest value is represented by the combination of ÔÇ£strategic framework plus specific sectoral mechanismsÔÇØ. The declaration sets the vector, and the applied agreements fill it with measurable content - with a target of bringing trade turnover to $1 billion.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><b>ÔÇö During the negotiations, the idea was discussed to connect the Baku-Tbilisi-Kars railway corridor with the China-Kyrgyzstan-Uzbekistan highway that is under construction. What factors today favor the implementation of this idea? And what obstacles can get in the way?<\\/b><\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">ÔÇö The idea of ÔÇïÔÇïconnecting the Baku-Tbilisi-Kars railway corridor with the China-Kyrgyzstan-Uzbekistan highway under construction has a clear strategic logic. Essentially, what we\'re talking about is<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">on the formation of a continuous railway route from China<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">to Europe, passing through Central Asia, the Caspian Sea and the South Caucasus, bypassing northern directions.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Among the favorable factors, I would highlight several. First of all, a practical move<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Project ode China - Kyrgyzstan -<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Uzbekistan is under construction: the investment agreement was signed in December 2024, and full-scale work<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">deployed in the Kyrgyz section from 2025.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">That is, the eastern ÔÇ£shoulderÔÇØ of the future corridor acquires physical<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">not the outlines. Baku highway -<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Tbi<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">fox<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">ÔÇö Kars, in turn, is already operating and integrated into the Middle Corridor, and Uzbekistan conducted pilot transit transportation along it several years ago. Finally, there is a coincidence of political will: interest<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">the route simultaneously demonstrates China, the countries of Central Asia, Azerbaijan, Georgia and T├╝rkiye, and the July visit itself showed that the Georgian direction is being considered by Tashkent more and more substantively.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">At the same time, the obstacles are serious and largely objective.<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">First<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">this is the infrastructural and technical complexity of the China-Kyrgyzstan-Uzbekistan highway itself: the route passes through the highlands with dozens of tunnels and bridges,<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">and construction is expected to take several years.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">The second is the problem of different track widths (1435 mm in China versus 1520 mm in the post-Soviet space), which requires transshipment operations and increases costs.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Third, the ÔÇ£connectingÔÇØ nature of the route: each crossing of the state border and each transshipment, including ferry crossings across the Caspian Sea, add time and cost. Finally, the commercial sustainability of the corridor will depend on the guaranteed<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">cargo base and a coordinated, competitive tariff policy throughout its entire length.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Poet<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">I assess this idea as strategically promising, but requiring step-by-step, painstaking work. Its implementation is not a matter of declarations, but of synchronizing infrastructure schedules, unifying technical standards and building a single tariff contour.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><b>ÔÇö Uzbekistan regularly declares its interest in transport and logistics<\\/b><\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><b>ical projects that will connect Central Asia with Europe through the South Caucasus. In your opinion, is there a need to launch a separate format ÔÇ£Central Asia - South CaucasusÔÇØ? Or are the existing bilateral and multilateral mechanisms sufficient for now?<\\/b><\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">ÔÇ£I believe that the objective prerequisites for the institutionalization of such interaction are really maturing. We are not observing isolated bilateral steps, but a steady trend of rapprochement between the two regions: in recent months<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Georgia has formalized a strategic partnership not only with Uzbekistan, but also with Kazakhstan, and intensified contacts with Tajikistan and Kyrgyzstan. This is no longer the sum of individual tracks, but an emerging system of connections with a common logistics and economic logic.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">At the same time, I would warn against hasty creation<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">the creation of another bureaucratic structure for the sake of the very fact of its existence. Today, interaction is fully operational based on a combination of mechanisms: bilateral intergovernmental commissions, the formats of the Middle Corridor and the Trans-Caspian Route,<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\"><br>&nbsp;<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">as well as platforms for dialogue with external partners - the European Union, Turkey, China. These tools have not yet exhausted their potential.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;mso-border-left-alt:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;mso-border-right-alt:none;mso-border-bottom-alt:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Therefore, my answer is balanced. At the current stage, it is more logical not to replace, but to complete the existing architecture - for example, through regular consultations at the level of specialized<\\/span><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">departments and coordination of transport and tariff policy. A separate format ÔÇ£Central Asia - South CaucasusÔÇØ will be justified when the density and complexity of connections exceed the capabilities of existing mechanisms and require a permanent coordinating circuit.<\\/span><\\/p><p style=\\\"text-indent:35.400000000000006pt;text-align:justify;line-height:114%;margin-top:6.000000000000001pt;margin-bottom:6.000000000000001pt;border:none;border-left:none;border-top:none;border-right:none;border-bottom:none;mso-border-between:none\\\"><span style=\\\"font-family:\'Cambria\';font-size:14pt;color:#000000;mso-style-textfill-fill-color:#000000\\\">Judging by the current dynamics, this moment is approaching, and it is worth preparing for it in advance - but the issue should be approached pragmatically, starting from the real content of cooperation, and not from the desire to multiply institutions.<\\/span><\\/p>\"}',23,'{\"uz\":\"\",\"ru\":\"\",\"en\":\"\"}');
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `publications` ENABLE KEYS */;
UNLOCK TABLES;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS `media`;
/*!40101 SET @saved_cs_client     = @@character_set_client */;
/*!40101 SET character_set_client = utf8mb4 */;
CREATE TABLE `media` (
  `id` varchar(40) NOT NULL,
  `type` varchar(40) DEFAULT NULL,
  `url` varchar(500) DEFAULT NULL,
  `title` longtext DEFAULT NULL,
  `date` date DEFAULT NULL,
  `photos` longtext DEFAULT NULL,
  `cover` varchar(500) DEFAULT NULL,
  `kind` varchar(40) DEFAULT NULL,
  `name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,
  `thumb` varchar(500) DEFAULT NULL,
  `seq` bigint(20) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,
  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),
  UNIQUE KEY `seq_idx` (`seq`),
  KEY `type` (`type`)
) ENGINE=InnoDB AUTO_INCREMENT=36 DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4 COLLATE=utf8mb4_unicode_ci;
/*!40101 SET character_set_client = @saved_cs_client */;

